add(dict(id=pid,title='Separate guest geometry, excitonic coupling and host response in cucurbituril complexes',objective='Test how host size and guest stoichiometry change emissive-state character, separating the contribution of confined guest geometry from interguest coupling and the explicit host response.',objects={'G1':obj('C34H28N2',2,(1,3)),'G1_2':obj('C68H56N4',4,(1,3)),'CB7':obj('C42H42N28O14',0,(1,)),'CB8':obj('C48H48N32O16',0,(1,)),'CB7_G1':obj('C76H70N30O14',2,(1,3)),'CB8_G1':obj('C82H76N34O16',2,(1,3)),'CB8_G1_2':obj('C116H104N36O16',4,(1,3))},keep_files=['g1_monomer_identity.json','g1_dimer_identity.json','g1_monomer.xyz','g1_dimer.xyz'],boundary='Use the original complete G1 dication identity and the supplied mapped neutral CB7/CB8 host graphs. Form 1:1 CB7:G1 and 1:2 CB8:G1 complexes. Generate independent host–guest placements; the added bound source coordinates are withheld. Counterions are excluded consistently. Use one aqueous continuum convention. Label each guest atom G_A/id or G_B/id and host H/id.',reference_definition='Compare transition energies/oscillator strengths and matched guest transition densities at identical guest coordinates for full-host, deleted-host and deleted-partner controls. Bright/dark splitting is E(bright)-E(dark) with both state identities recorded; host deletion measures combined host response, not purely electrostatics.',controls={'bound_starts':'Use at least two relative guest orientations/placements for each host stoichiometry. Ring topology is fixed by the host graph; no optimized complex is supplied.','host_deletion':'At each bound geometry remove all host atoms without relaxing guest coordinates; retain guest charges +2/+4 and the same aqueous embedding.','partner_deletion':'For CB8:(G1)2 additionally remove guest B then guest A, giving the two +2 single guests at their frozen bound positions, both with and without CB8.','free_guests':'Relax isolated G1 and G1_2 independently in the same solvent. Compare confinement-induced geometry and excitonic coupling separately.','state_character':'Define fragments host/guestA/guestB before comparing NTOs; inspect whether host-centered or charge-transfer states enter the window.'},panels={'host_matrix':panel(K(['CB7_G1','CB8_G1_2'],K(['full_host','deleted_host'],hcell)),'Explicit-host versus same-geometry deleted-host controls','Verify chemical stoichiometry/charge and exact guest-coordinate identity. Compare actual TD/density outputs and states, not labels assigned from an emission cartoon.'),'coupling_controls':panel(O(CB8_partner_A_with_host=hcell,CB8_partner_B_with_host=hcell,CB8_partner_A_no_host=hcell,CB8_partner_B_no_host=hcell,free_G1=hcell,free_G1_2=hcell,placement_attempt_records=REF),'Partner deletion and relaxed free-guest baselines','Single guests have charge +2; two guests +4. Keep host and retained guest coordinates fixed on deletion. For monomers bright/dark are the selected lowest optically active/inactive states, not fictitious exciton partners; explicitly state if no suitable dark state occurs in the calculated window.'),'mechanism_comparison':panel(K(['host_effect_CB7','host_effect_CB8','guest_coupling_CB8','confinement_geometry_effect'],PAIR),'Host response versus guest coupling attribution','Require density-based state correspondence and quantitative differences. An isolated dimer splitting alone cannot establish host causality. Full-host deletion includes electrostatics, polarization and exchange; pure electrostatic attribution requires an additional frozen-charge control, which is optional.')},investigations={'host_matrix':'Build and validate both complete host–guest systems from independent placements; calculate state-resolved optical properties before and after frozen host deletion.','coupling_controls':'Perform the four frozen partner-deletion controls and relaxed isolated monomer/dimer baselines. Preserve mappings, placements, raw TD vectors and transition densities.','mechanism_comparison':'Compare host response, guest–guest coupling and geometric confinement with a common state definition, and quantify one basis/solvent/placement sensitivity.'},author='Main pp2–4 and formal SI Section B identify CB7:G1=1:1 and CB8:G1=1:2; the source interprets host-dependent emission using H-type guest aggregation and guest conformation. Computations use Gaussian16 B3LYP-D3/6-31G(d), PCM water. SI Section D gives source optimized coordinates and transition data; these remain private. The deleted-host, deleted-partner and matched-state interventions are new and may refute a simple H-aggregate explanation.',pitfalls='Reject wrong host ring size/guest charge, source bound coordinates used as discovered minima, comparisons with relaxed versus frozen geometries silently mixed, or calling total host response exclusively electrostatic. Static transition properties do not prove fluorescence quantum yield.',optional='CB10, full concentration titration, explicit-water dynamics, pure electrostatic embedding and absolute emission efficiencies are optional.',source_note='Read main pp2–4 and publisher SI Sections B (NMR stoichiometry), C (photophysics) and D (computational structures/TableS2). Extracted host-only graphs from 190-/272-atom complexes; verified CB7 C42H42N28O14 and CB8 C48H48N32O16. No new bound coordinates or TD outputs were published.',software='Gaussian or ORCA TDDFT, xTB/CREST for placement prescreening if compatible with host charge, Multiwfn transition-density analysis.',pilot='Verify one neutral CB host graph and G1 charge; relax a CB7:G1 placement and test the matched full/deleted host TD calculation before CB8.',reference_gaps=['No new host/partner-deletion reference or placement/spectral uncertainty is computed.','TD state windows and dark-state identification need calibration for both bound stoichiometries.']))

