add(dict(id=pid,title='Test heavy-atom excited-state coupling and rubrene transfer energetics',objective='Compare actual state character and SOC in the three sensitizers and determine whether a consistently defined rubrene energy-transfer cycle supports their proposed sensitization role.',objects={k:obj(f,q,(1,3),identity_file=k+'_identity.json') for k,f,q in [('IR780','C36H44ClN2',1),('Cy1','C34H38ClI2N2',1),('Cy2','C27H29N2O2Se2',1),('rubrene','C42H28',0)]},boundary='Use complete supplied mapped graphs, monocationic sensitizers with omitted iodide counterions, and separate neutral rubrene. The sensitizers differ in backbone and substituents as well as heavy atoms; they are not an isostructural I-to-Se substitution series. Use common chloroform continuum conditions and explicit heavy-element relativistic/basis definitions.',reference_definition='Matched vertical gaps and SOC are evaluated at each S0 geometry. For energy transfer use consistent adiabatic excitation energies: delta_TTET=E_T1(rubrene)-E_T1(sensitizer); negative is energetically downhill. Rubrene annihilation balance=2E_T1(rubrene)-E_S1(rubrene); nonnegative is an energetic prerequisite. These are not rates or quantum yields.',controls={'states':'For each sensitizer calculate S1/S2 and T1/T2 at minimum, enlarging roots to establish state character and adjacent-state competition; report actual SOC, not atomic Z scaling.','geometry':'Use Cy2 as fixed representative: freeze its mapped conjugated-chain dihedrals to planar 180/0 degree values according to alternating bond orientation and compare with relaxed geometry; release constraints and validate. Record atom quadruples and achieved angles.','rubrene':'Independently optimize rubrene S0,T1,S1 with root following, and all three sensitizer T1 states; no Cy2 self-TTA substitution.','relativity':'Repeat decisive Cy2 SOC with a justified basis/relativistic treatment sensitivity. A spin-free ECP needs a supported SOC operator, not an assumed scalar-only conversion.'},panels={'sensitizer_states':panel(K(['IR780','Cy1','Cy2'],exc),'Three sensitizer low-state windows and actual SOC','Check complete graphs, cation charge and hole/electron localization on heavy atoms. Compare physically matched states; chemistry differences forbid attributing all changes exclusively to atom mass or position.'),'rubrene_energy_cycle':panel(O(rubrene_S0_record=S(),rubrene_S1_record=S(),rubrene_T1_record=S(),rubrene_E_S1_eV=N(),rubrene_E_T1_eV=N(),annihilation_balance_eV=N(),sensitizers=K(['IR780','Cy1','Cy2'],O(T1_record=S(),E_T1_eV=N(),delta_TTET_eV=N(),evidence_files=EV)),state_following_file=PATH,evidence_files=EV),'Independent acceptor energies and closed transfer/annihilation comparison','Recompute rubrene and donor adiabatic energies under matching conditions. Do not subtract vertical donor states from adiabatic acceptor states or use different solvent corrections silently.'),'geometry_and_method_test':panel(O(Cy2_frozen=exc,Cy2_released_record=S(),torsion_mapping_file=PATH,geometry_effect=PAIR,relativistic_basis_effect=PAIR,heavy_atom_density_file=PATH,evidence_files=EV),'Geometry versus heavy-atom coupling and method robustness','Verify real SOC vectors, supported heavy-element operators, frozen geometry and state density overlap. More SOC or favorable TTET energetics does not by itself demonstrate a faster ISC/transfer process.')},investigations={'sensitizer_states':'Generate independent conformers for all sensitizers, validate S0 and calculate state-resolved energy, oscillator/NTO and SOC windows with root tracking.','rubrene_energy_cycle':'Calculate independent rubrene S1/T1 and sensitizer T1 adiabatic energies and form the stated TTET and acceptor-annihilation balances.','geometry_and_method_test':'Run the Cy2 planar/released intervention and a real SOC-method sensitivity; assess localized heavy-atom character and scope of any mechanistic inference.'},author='Main pp4–6 and SI pp7–8 use Gaussian16 B3LYP/6-31G(d,p), SMD chloroform; Se uses def2-TZVP and I uses SDD, with iodide removed. The authors propose heavy-atom-assisted sensitization and discuss Cy2 and rubrene upconversion. The benchmark requires actual SOC and an independent acceptor energy cycle, which are not replaced by qualitative atomic-number arguments or the old isolated Cy2 energies.',pitfalls='Reject wrong cation/counterion model, scalar-relativistic energy mistaken for SOC, Cy2 used as the annihilator instead of rubrene, mixed vertical/adiabatic references, or full efficiency/position-only causation from non-isostructural compounds.',optional='Cy3, absolute ISC/TTET/TTA kinetics, diffusion, solution aggregation and device/quantum-yield predictions are optional.',source_note='Read main pp4–6; SI pp7–8 method, source S0/S1/T1/T2 coordinate sections pp24–30. Derived only IR780/Cy1/Cy2 graph identities; verified rubrene named graph C42H28. Formulas show backbone differences, so a simple I-versus-Se matched causal claim is not supported by identities.',software='ORCA TDDFT/SOC with supported relativistic basis/operator; Gaussian for source-condition energies; Multiwfn transition analysis.',pilot='Verify Cy2 heavy-element SOC and root tracking, then rubrene T1/S1 identity before expanding three sensitizers.',reference_gaps=['New SOC, independent rubrene energetics, geometry intervention and relativistic uncertainty are uncomputed.']))

